(setq register-alist '((114) (48 . #("\\subsection{(Un)decidable Problems about Reachability of Quantum\n  Systems~\\cite{li_decidable_2014}}\n\n\\subsubsection{Basic Definitions and Main Results}\n\\paragraph{A Propositional Logic for Quantum Systems}\nLet $\\HH$ be a Hilbert space: a real or complex inner product space that is\nalso a complete metric space with respect to the distance induced by the\ninner product. For example,\n\\[\n\\HH=\\mathbb C^2=\\spn\\{\\ket0,\\ket1\\}.\n\\]\nA basic property of the system can be described by a closed subspace\n$V$ of $\\HH$. For example, $V=\\spn\\{\\ket0\\}\\subseteq\\HH$.\nA linear subspace is closed if it contains limits: if $\\ket{\\psi_n}\\in V$\nand $\\|\\ket{\\psi_n}-\\ket\\psi\\|\\to0$, then $\\ket\\psi\\in V$.\nIf $V=\\spn\\{\\ket{v_1},\\ldots,\\ket{v_n}\\}$ with an orthonormal basis, then\n\\[\nP_V=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ket{v_i}\\bra{v_i}.\n\\]\nTo check whether the property is satisfied, perform a binary (yes--no)\nmeasurement $\\{P_V,P_{V^\\perp}\\}$ on the current state $\\ket\\psi$.\nThese are the projections onto $V$ and its orthogonal complement.\n\n\n\\subparagraph*{Probabilistic satisfaction}\nFor normalized $\\ket\\psi$, $V$ is satisfied with probability\n$\\bra\\psi P_V\\ket\\psi$, and is not satisfied with probability\n\\[\n\\bra\\psi P_{V^\\perp}\\ket\\psi=1-\\bra\\psi P_V\\ket\\psi.\n\\]\n\\subparagraph*{Quantitative satisfaction}\nFor $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ and $\\triangleright\\in\\{<,\\leq,>,\\geq\\}$,\n$V$ is $(\\lambda,\\triangleright)$-satisfied in $\\ket\\psi$ if\n\\[\n\\bra\\psi P_V\\ket\\psi\\mathrel{\\triangleright}\\lambda.\n\\]\n\\subparagraph*{Qualitative satisfaction}\nThe threshold $\\lambda$ is $0$ or $1$. The notes record\n\\begin{align*}\nV&=\\{\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH:V\\text{ is }(1,\\geq)\\text{-satisfied in }\\ket\\psi\\},\\\\\nV^\\perp&=\\{\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH:V\\text{ is }(0,\\leq)\\text{-satisfied in }\\ket\\psi\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nThese probability characterizations apply to normalized states.\nThe first equality is not literally an equality of sets of all vectors:\na linear subspace includes unnormalized vectors and zero.\n\n\\begin{example}\n  We review the following example to illustrate satisfiability in the quantum\n  setting. For a normalized state  $\\ket{\\psi}$, we\n  have $\\ket{\\psi} \\models V \\Leftrightarrow \\bra{\\psi} P_V \\ket{\\psi} = 1\n  \\Leftrightarrow \\ket{\\psi} \\in V$.\n\n  Consider $V = \\Span\\{\\ket{0}\\}$.  If\n  $\\ket{\\psi} = \\sqrt{0.99} \\ket{0} + \\sqrt{0.01} \\ket{1}$, then $\\ket{\\psi}$\n  does not satisfy $V$, despite a $99\\%$ chance of measuring a $\\ket{0}$ state.\n\n  This is because measuring $\\ket{\\psi}$ using the measurement $P_V =\n  \\ket{0}\\bra{0}$ we obtain $\\frac{\\ket{P_V \\ket{\\psi}}}{\\sqrt{\\bra{\\psi}\n      P_V^{\\dag} P_V \\ket{\\psi}}} = \\ket{0}$.\n\\end{example}\n\n\\subparagraph*{Atomic propositions}\n\\[\n\\AP=\\{V:V\\text{ is a closed subspace of }\\HH\\}.\n\\]\n\n\nLogical formulas are generated from $\\AP$ using Boolean connectives.\nFor $\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH$:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\ket\\psi\\models f&\\iff\\ket\\psi\\in f\\qquad(f\\in\\AP),\\\\\n\\ket\\psi\\models\\neg f&\\iff\\ket\\psi\\not\\models f,\\\\\n\\ket\\psi\\models f_1\\land f_2\n&\\iff\\ket\\psi\\models f_1\\text{ and }\\ket\\psi\\models f_2,\\\\\n\\ket\\psi\\models f_1\\lor f_2\n&\\iff\\ket\\psi\\models f_1\\text{ or }\\ket\\psi\\models f_2.\n\\end{align*}\nWrite\n\\[\n\\sem f=\\{\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH:\\ket\\psi\\models f\\}.\n\\]\nThis need not be a subspace. For $V=\\spn\\{\\ket0\\}\\subseteq\\mathbb C^2$,\nand normalized states,\n\\[\n\\sem{\\neg V}=\\{\\ket\\psi:V\\text{ is }(1,<)\\text{-satisfied in }\\ket\\psi\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince $V$ is a linear subspace, we have $\\textbf{0} \\in V$ so $\\textbf{0}\n\\models V$. By definition of negation, we have $\\ket\\psi \\models \\neg V$ if and\nonly if $\\ket\\psi \\not\\models V$; Hence, $\\textbf{0} \\not\\in \\sem{\\neg V}$.\n\n\\paragraph{Reachability of Quantum Automata}\n\\textbf{Definition 1.} A quantum automaton is\n\\[\n\\A=(\\HH,\\Act,\\{U_\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Act\\},\\HH_{\\ini}),\n\\]\nwhere:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item $\\HH$ is the state Hilbert space;\n\\item $\\Act$ is a finite set of actions of processes;\n\\item $U_\\alpha$ is a unitary operator on $\\HH$ for each $\\alpha\\in\\Act$;\n\\item $\\HH_{\\ini}\\subseteq\\HH$ is the subspace of initial states.\n\\end{enumerate}\nIt is finite-dimensional if $\\HH$ is finite-dimensional. Only\nfinite-dimensional quantum automata are considered.\n\n\\subparagraph*{Language accepted by a quantum automaton}\n\nFor $s=\\alpha_0\\cdots\\alpha_n\\in\\Act^*$, define\n\\[\nU_s=U_{\\alpha_n}\\cdots U_{\\alpha_1}U_{\\alpha_0}.\n\\]\nThe notation $\\Act^*$ denotes finite strings.\nIf $U_s\\ket{\\psi_0}\\models f$ for some initial state\n$\\ket{\\psi_0}\\in \\HH_{\\ini}$, then $s$ is accepted by $\\A$ with $f$.\nAll such strings form the language $\\Lang(\\A,f)$.\n\n\\subparagraph*{Real-time quantum automata}\n\nFollowing Moore and Crutchfield~\\cite{moore_quantum_1997},\na real-time quantum automaton $Q$ consists of:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item A Hilbert space $H$.\n    \\item An initial state $s_{\\mathrm{init}}\\in H$\n    satisfying $\\|s_{\\mathrm{init}}\\|^2=1$.\n    \\item An accepting subspace\n    $H_{\\mathrm{accept}}\\subseteq H$ with\n    orthogonal projector $P_{\\mathrm{accept}}$.\n    \\item An input alphabet $A$.\n    \\item A unitary transition operator $U_a$\n    for each $a\\in A$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nFor an input word $w=w_1\\cdots w_n\\in A^*$, let\n\\[\n    U_w=U_{w_1}\\cdots U_{w_n},\n    \\qquad U_\\varepsilon=I.\n\\]\nThe quantum language recognized by $Q$ is the function\n\\[\n    f_Q:A^*\\longrightarrow[0,1],\n    \\qquad\n    f_Q(w)=\n    \\bigl\\|s_{\\mathrm{init}}U_wP_{\\mathrm{accept}}\\bigr\\|^2.\n\\]\n\nHere, following the authors' convention, states are represented\nas row vectors and operators act on the right. Thus, the symbols\nof $w$ are processed in left-to-right order. The value $f_Q(w)$\nis the probability of obtaining an accepting outcome in a\nmeasurement performed after processing the entire word.\nThe automaton is real-time because it performs exactly one\ntransition per input symbol, with no additional computation\nafter the input has been read. The definition does not require\n$H$ to be finite-dimensional; imposing this restriction yields\na quantum finite-state automaton.\n\n\\subparagraph*{Comparison of acceptance conventions.}\nIf $f$ in the notation $L(\\mathcal{A},f)$ denotes an accepting\nsubspace, then it plays the same role as\n$H_{\\mathrm{accept}}$ above. In Moore and Crutchfield's\nformulation, the accepting subspace is included in the\nspecification of the automaton. In a formulation using\n$L(\\mathcal{A},f)$, the acceptance condition may instead be\nsupplied separately through $f$.\n\nThis correspondence between accepting subspaces does not,\nby itself, establish equivalence of the recognized languages.\nMoore and Crutchfield define a probability-valued function\n$f_Q$, whereas an ordinary language\n$L(\\mathcal{A},f)\\subseteq A^*$ additionally requires a rule\ndetermining membership from the final state or its acceptance\nprobability. Moreover, the notation should be distinguished:\n$f_Q$ is a function on words, while $f$ in\n$L(\\mathcal{A},f)$ is, under the stated assumption, a subspace.\n\n\\subparagraph*{Paths and Reachability Properties}\n\nChoose $\\ket{\\psi_0}\\in \\HH_{\\ini}$ and $\\alpha_n\\in\\Act$, with\n$\\ket{\\psi_{n+1}}=U_{\\alpha_n}\\ket{\\psi_n}$ for all $n\\geq0$. A path is\n\\[\np=\\ket{\\psi_0}\\xrightarrow{U_{\\alpha_0}}\\ket{\\psi_1}\n\\xrightarrow{U_{\\alpha_1}}\\ket{\\psi_2}\n\\xrightarrow{U_{\\alpha_2}}\\cdots.\n\\]\nA schedule is an infinite sequence\n$w=\\alpha_0\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\cdots\\in\\Act^\\omega$.\nWrite $p=p(\\ket{\\psi_0},w)$ for the corresponding path and\n\\[\n\\sigma(p)=\\ket{\\psi_0}\\ket{\\psi_1}\\ket{\\psi_2}\\cdots\n\\]\nfor its sequence of states; this is often also called a path.\n\nLet $f$ be a Boolean combination of subspaces and\n$\\sigma=\\ket{\\psi_0}\\ket{\\psi_1}\\cdots$ an infinite sequence in $\\HH$.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sigma\\models Ff&\\iff\\exists i\\geq0:\\ket{\\psi_i}\\models f\n&&\\text{(eventually reachable)},\\\\\n\\sigma\\models Gf&\\iff\\forall i\\geq0:\\ket{\\psi_i}\\models f\n&&\\text{(globally reachable)},\\\\\n\\sigma\\models Uf&\\iff\\allinf i\\geq0:\\ket{\\psi_i}\\models f\n&&\\text{(ultimately forever reachable)},\\\\\n\\sigma\\models If&\\iff\\exinf i\\geq0:\\ket{\\psi_i}\\models f\n&&\\text{(infinitely often reachable)}.\n\\end{align*}\nHere,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\allinf i\\geq0:P(i)&\\iff\\exists j\\geq0\\;\\forall i\\geq j:P(i),\\\\\n\\exinf i\\geq0:P(i)&\\iff\\forall j\\geq0\\;\\exists i\\geq j:P(i).\n\\end{align*}\nThe intuitions are \\emph{eventually always} and \\emph{infinitely often}\nrespectively.\n\nThe authors define a language of finite action strings through satisfaction of a\nstate formula after execution. Infinite schedules generate the executions on\nwhich temporal reachability properties are interpreted; they do not change\n\\(L(\\mathcal A,f)\\) into an infinite-word language.\n\n\\textbf{Definition 2.} For $\\Delta\\in\\{F,G,U,I\\}$,\n\\[\n\\A\\models\\Delta f\\iff\n\\sigma(p)\\models\\Delta f\\text{ for all paths }p\\text{ in }\\A.\n\\]\n\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\\toprule\nQuantifier & Intuition\\\\\\midrule\n$\\forall i:P(i)$ & Always holds; no exceptions.\\\\\n$\\allinf i:P(i)$ & Only finitely many exceptions.\\\\\n$\\exinf i:P(i)$ & Infinitely many successes; failures may also be infinite.\\\\\n\\bottomrule\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}\n\nFor $S\\subseteq\\Act^*$, define\n\\[\n\\omega(S)=\\{w=\\alpha_0\\alpha_1\\cdots\\in\\Act^\\omega:\n\\exinf n\\geq0,\\ \\alpha_0\\cdots\\alpha_n\\in S\\}.\n\\]\nThe set $S$ satisfies \\emph{liveness} if $\\omega(S)=\\Act^\\omega$ and\n\\emph{deadness} if $\\omega(S)=\\varnothing$.\n\nLet $P_w(n)$ mean that the prefix $\\alpha_0\\cdots\\alpha_n$ is in $S$.\n\\begin{description}\n\\item[Liveness:] $\\forall w\\;\\exinf n:P_w(n)$.\nEvery schedule has infinitely many prefixes in $S$.\n\\item[Deadness:] $\\forall w\\;\\allinf n:\\neg P_w(n)$.\nEvery schedule eventually has no more prefixes in $S$.\n\\end{description}\n\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.} Let $\\dim H_{\\ini}=1$. Then:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item $\\A\\models Ff$ iff\n$\\Act^\\omega=\\Lang(\\A,f)\\cdot\\Act^\\omega$, where the dot means concatenation.\n\\item $\\A\\models If$ iff $\\Lang(\\A,f)$ satisfies liveness.\n\\item $\\A\\models Gf$ iff $\\Lang(\\A,f)=\\Act^*$, equivalently\n$\\Lang(\\A,\\neg f)=\\varnothing$.\n\\item $\\A\\models Uf$ iff\n$\\Act^*\\setminus\\Lang(\\A,f)=\\Lang(\\A,\\neg f)$ satisfies deadness.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subparagraph*{Theorem statement}\n\\textbf{Theorem 2 (Decidability).}\nIf $\\Delta\\in\\{G,U,I\\}$ and $f$ has no negations, deciding whether\n$\\A\\models\\Delta f$ is decidable.\n\n\n\\setcounter{subsubsection}{4}\n\\subsubsection{Decidable Results}\nIf $f$ has no negations, write it in disjunctive normal form:\n\\[\nf=\\bigvee_{i=1}^m f_i.\n\\]\nEach $f_i$ is a conjunction of subspace propositions, so $\\sem{f_i}$ is a\nsubspace of $\\HH$ (closed subspaces are closed under intersection).\nWrite $V_i=\\sem{f_i}\\in\\AP$. Then equivalently\n\\[\nf=\\bigvee_{i=1}^m V_i,\\qquad \\sem f=\\bigcup_{i=1}^m V_i,\n\\]\na union of finitely many subspaces of $\\HH$.\n\nTo decide $\\A\\models\\Delta f$, compute all predecessor states for the property.\nGiven\n\\[\n\\A=(\\HH,\\Act,\\{U_\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Act\\},\\HH_{\\ini}),\\quad\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH,\n\\]\ndefine\n\\[\n\\A(\\psi)=(\\HH,\\Act,\\{U_\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Act\\},\\spn\\{\\ket\\psi\\}).\n\\]\n\n\nFor $\\Delta\\in\\{G,U,I\\}$, call $\\ket\\psi$ a $(\\Delta,f)$-predecessor state\nif $\\A(\\psi)\\models\\Delta f$. Define\n\\[\nY(\\A,\\Delta,f)=\\{\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH:\\A(\\psi)\\models\\Delta f\\}.\n\\]\nThis ignores the original $\\HH_{\\ini}$ of $\\A$. Then\n\\[\n\\A\\models\\Delta f\\iff \\HH_{\\ini}\\subseteq Y(\\A,\\Delta,f).\n\\]\n\\paragraph{Decidability of $\\A\\models If$ for a Single Unitary Operator}\nWrite $Y=Y(\\A,I,f)$. Consider $|\\Act|=1$ and $m=1$, so $f=V$ is a subspace.\nLet $\\Act=\\{\\alpha\\}$ and represent the string $\\alpha^n$ by $n$.\n\n\\textbf{Approach.} Show algorithmically that $Y$ is a union of finitely many\nsubspaces $Y_0,\\ldots,Y_{p-1}$ forming a cycle:\n\\[\nY_{r+1}=U_\\alpha Y_r\\quad(0\\leq r\\leq p-2),\\qquad\nY_0=U_\\alpha Y_{p-1}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nY=\\bigcup_{r=0}^{p-1}U_\\alpha^rY_0,\\qquad Y_0=U_\\alpha^pY_0.\n\\]\n\n\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.} For any unitary $U$ on $\\HH$, a positive integer $p$ can be\nfound such that, for every subspace $K$ of $\\HH$,\n\\[\n(\\exists n>0:\\ U^nK=K)\\Longrightarrow U^pK=K.\n\\]\nCall $p$ a \\emph{period of $U$}. In quantifier notation:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\exists p\\in\\mathbb N_{>0}\\;\\forall K\\text{ a subspace of }\\HH:\n(\\exists n>0,\\ U^nK=K)\\Longrightarrow U^pK=K.}\n\\]\nHere $U^nK=\\{U^nv:v\\in K\\}$.\n\n\\textbf{Intuition:} a common return time for every subspace that ever\nreturns to itself.\n\\subparagraph*{Algorithm 1}\n\\textbf{Input:} $V\\subseteq\\HH$ encoding $f$, and $U_\\alpha$\n(the only required dynamics information from $\\A$ in this case).\n\n\\textbf{Output:} $Y=Y(\\A,I,f)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Compute a period $p$ of $U_\\alpha$ using Lemma 2.\n\\item Compute the maximal subspace $K$ of $V$ with $U_\\alpha^pK=K$.\n\\item Return $Y=\\bigcup_{r=0}^{p-1}U_\\alpha^rK$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.} If $p$ and $K$ are obtained by Algorithm 1, then\n\\[\nY=\\bigcup_{r=0}^{p-1}U_\\alpha^rK.\n\\]\nCheck $\\HH_{\\ini}\\subseteq Y$ by testing $\\HH_{\\ini}\\subseteq U_\\alpha^rK$.\n\nPrecisely, at least one component must contain the whole initial subspace.\nContainment in a finite union of complex subspaces implies containment in one\ncomponent.\n\n\\paragraph{Decidability of $\\A\\models If$ for the General Case}\nNow construct $Y=Y(\\A,I,f)$ for multiple actions and\n$f=\\bigvee_{i=1}^mV_i$. The approach is to prove $Y$ is a finite union\nof subspaces.\n\n\\textbf{Lemma 4.} Let $X=\\{Y_1,\\ldots,Y_q\\}$ be subspaces of $\\HH$ such that:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item For any $Y_i$ and $\\alpha\\in\\Act$, some $Y_j$ satisfies $U_\\alpha Y_i=Y_j$.\nInterpret the subspaces as nodes and these transformations as edges, a graph\nmore general than the previous cycle.\n\\item For any simple loop\n\\[\nY_{r_0}\\xrightarrow{U_{\\alpha_0}}Y_{r_1}\n\\xrightarrow{U_{\\alpha_1}}\\cdots\n\\xrightarrow{U_{\\alpha_{k-2}}}Y_{r_{k-1}}\n\\xrightarrow{U_{\\alpha_{k-1}}}Y_{r_0},\n\\]\nsome $i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,k-1\\}$ and $j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ satisfy\n$Y_{r_i}\\subseteq V_j$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\n\n\\begin{enumerate}[start=3]\n\\item $Y\\subseteq Y_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup Y_q$.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThen $Y=Y_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup Y_q$.\nThus it suffices to construct a collection $X$ satisfying these conditions. The\nfollowing lemma was proved in~\\cite{li_termination_2012}.\n\n\\textbf{Lemma 5.}~\\label{lemma5} Suppose each $X_k$ is a finite union of\nsubspaces of $\\HH$.  If $X_0\\supseteq X_1\\supseteq\\cdots$, then there exists\n$n\\geq0$ such that $X_k=X_n$ for all $k\\geq n$.\n\n\\subparagraph*{Algorithm 2}\\label{algorithm2}\n\\textbf{Input:} a quantum automaton $\\A$ and a finite union $\\bigcup_iV_i$\nencoding $f$.\n\n\\textbf{Output:} a collection $X$ satisfying Lemma 4, with $\\bigcup X=Y$.\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Initialize $X\\leftarrow\\{\\HH\\}$.\n\\item If $X$ satisfies conditions 1 and 2 of Lemma 4, return $X$.\n\\item Otherwise construct a new finite collection $X'$ of subspaces with\n$Y\\subseteq\\bigcup X'\\subsetneq\\bigcup X$.\n\\item Set $X\\leftarrow X'$ and repeat step 2.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nAssuming refinement is computable, Algorithm 2 terminates by Lemma 5 and\nreturns the right answer by Lemma 4. Refinement is justified by:\n\n\\textbf{Lemma 6.} Given $X=\\{Y_1,\\ldots,Y_q\\}$ such that:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item no two distinct subspaces include one another;\n\\item condition 3 of Lemma 4 holds;\n\\item condition 1 or condition 2 of Lemma 4 fails,\n\\end{itemize}\nwe can algorithmically find some $Y_i\\in X$ and proper subspaces\n$W_1,\\ldots,W_\\ell$ of it with\n\\[\nY\\cap Y_i\\subseteq W_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup W_\\ell.\\tag{9}\n\\]\nUse this lemma to replace a member of $X$ by its proper subspaces.\nCondition (9) keeps condition 3 as a loop invariant.\nExplicitly, after removing members contained in other members, set\n\\[\nX'=(X\\setminus\\{Y_i\\})\\cup\\{W_1,\\ldots,W_\\ell\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Decidability of $\\A\\models Gf$ and $\\A\\models Uf$}\nFirst compute $Y=Y(\\A,G,f)$. By definition and clause 3 of Lemma 1:\n\\begin{align*}\nY(\\A,G,f)&=\\{\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH:\\A(\\psi)\\models Gf\\}\\\\\n&=\\{\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH:\\Lang(\\A(\\psi),f)=\\Act^*\\}\\\\\n&=\\{\\ket\\psi\\in\\HH:U_s\\ket\\psi\\in\\sem f\\text{ for all }s\\in\\Act^*\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nWe have $Y\\subseteq\\sem f$ because the empty string $s=\\epsilon$ has\n$U_\\epsilon=I$. Hence\n\\[\n\\ket\\psi\\in Y\\Longrightarrow I\\ket\\psi=\\ket\\psi\\in\\sem f.\n\\]\nAlso $U_\\alpha Y\\subseteq Y$ for any $\\alpha\\in\\Act$. Indeed, if\n$\\ket\\phi=U_\\alpha\\ket\\psi$ with $\\ket\\psi\\in Y$, then for any $s\\in\\Act^*$,\n\\[\nU_s\\ket\\phi=U_sU_\\alpha\\ket\\psi=U_{\\alpha s}\\ket\\psi\\in\\sem f.\n\\]\nThis holds because $\\alpha s$ is a finite action string and $\\ket\\psi\\in Y$.\nThus $\\ket\\phi\\in Y$, and\n\\[\n\\boxed{U_\\alpha Y\\subseteq Y\\subseteq\\sem f\\quad(\\alpha\\in\\Act).}\n\\]\n\nThe set $Y$ can be computed using the following algorithm.\n\n\n\\subparagraph*{Algorithm 3}\n\\textbf{Input:} a quantum automaton $\\A$ and subspaces $V_1,\\ldots,V_m$\nencoding $f$.\n\n\\textbf{Output:} $Y=Y(\\A,G,f)$.\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Set $Y\\leftarrow V_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup V_m$.\n\\item If $U_\\alpha Y\\neq Y$ for some $\\alpha\\in\\Act$, set\n\\[\nY\\leftarrow U_\\alpha^{-1}Y\\cap Y\n\\]\nand repeat step 2. Otherwise, return $Y$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Lemma 7.} For the positive formula under consideration,\n\\[\n\\A\\models Uf\\iff \\HH_{\\ini}\\subseteq Y(\\A,G,f).\n\\]\n\nHERE\n\n%%% Local Variables:\n%%% mode: latex\n%%% TeX-master: \"../main.tex\"\n%%% End:" 0 11 (face (font-lock-keyword-face font-latex-sedate-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 11 12 (font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 12 75 (face (font-latex-sectioning-3-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 75 80 (font-lock-multiline t face (font-lock-keyword-face font-latex-sectioning-3-face font-latex-sedate-face) fontified t) 80 81 (face (font-latex-sectioning-3-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 81 98 (font-lock-multiline t face (font-lock-constant-face font-latex-sectioning-3-face) fontified t) 98 99 (face (font-latex-sectioning-3-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 99 100 (font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 100 102 (fontified t) 102 116 (face (font-lock-keyword-face font-latex-sedate-face) fontified t) 116 117 (fontified t) 117 151 (face (font-latex-sectioning-4-face) fontified t) 151 153 (fontified t) 153 163 (face (font-lock-keyword-face font-latex-sedate-face) fontified t) 163 164 (fontified t) 164 205 (face (font-latex-sectioning-5-face) fontified t) 205 211 (fontified t) 211 212 (face font-latex-math-face fontified t) 212 215 (face (font-latex-math-face font-latex-sedate-face) fontified t) 215 216 (face font-latex-math-face fontified t) 216 384 (fontified t) 384 387 (face (font-latex-math-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 387 390 (face (font-latex-math-face font-latex-sedate-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 390 391 (face (font-latex-math-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 391 398 (face (font-latex-math-face font-latex-sedate-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 398 400 (face (font-latex-math-face) font-lock-multiline t fontified t) 400 401 (face (font-latex-script-char-face font-lat